1
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:13,701
Last time, the whole of Shiloh cried out at once, and the blind man by the road was
the last to be told why.

2
00:00:13,751 --> 00:00:19,410
Now the same town, about a hundred and seventy years on, and quiet.

3
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:31,710
Imagine a house in Shiloh, a long time ago. The old man inside cannot see. Someone
has just stopped at his door, and has not said anything yet.

4
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:42,870
[Ahijah] Come in, wife of Jeroboam. Why are you disguised?

5
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,771
She has not spoken. He has not seen her.

6
00:00:47,821 --> 00:00:57,558
She is the queen of Israel, disguised so that no one on the way would know her,
carrying bread and cakes and a jar of honey,

7
00:00:57,608 --> 00:01:01,346
and the first word out of the house is her name.

8
00:01:01,396 --> 00:01:07,710
Here is Brian, by way of Mister Scaredy Cat, on what a blind man hears at a door.

9
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:16,803
[Brian] I can tell Barb's sound if she wears certain shoes, but often, in places
without context, it is hard to pick out her footsteps among others.

10
00:01:16,853 --> 00:01:24,975
[Brian] So I want to be honest about this before we start. Ahijah did not recognize
her footsteps. The text does not say he did, and I could not have.

11
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:31,050
[Brian] What he had was better than good ears. God had already told him who was
coming and that she would be in disguise.

12
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:38,012
[Brian] So when feet stopped at his door, he did not have to work out whose they
were. He already knew. That is the difference.

13
00:01:38,062 --> 00:01:42,485
[Brian] In the other stories the blind man has to figure out who is there from what
he can hear.

14
00:01:42,535 --> 00:01:50,177
[Brian] Here the blind man is told ahead of time, and the footsteps only tell him
she has arrived. That is what this story is about to me.

15
00:01:50,227 --> 00:01:55,190
[Brian] The one time in the series a blind man did not have to work anything out,
because he had already been told.

16
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:08,848
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

17
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:17,282
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

18
00:02:17,332 --> 00:02:20,200
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

19
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:32,911
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

20
00:02:32,961 --> 00:02:48,523
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

21
00:02:48,573 --> 00:02:54,206
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

22
00:02:54,256 --> 00:03:07,212
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

23
00:03:07,262 --> 00:03:14,580
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

24
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:22,646
First, where we are. Solomon is dead and the kingdom has split in two.

25
00:03:22,696 --> 00:03:33,051
Years before that, when Jeroboam was only one of Solomon's officers, a prophet from
Shiloh named Ahijah met him alone on the road out of Jerusalem,

26
00:03:33,101 --> 00:03:38,289
took hold of the new cloak he was wearing, and tore it into twelve pieces.

27
00:03:38,339 --> 00:03:42,880
First Kings chapter 11, verses 29 to 31, and then 38.

28
00:03:43,930 --> 00:03:52,456
[Reader] During that time, the prophet Ahijah the Shilonite met Jeroboam on the road
as he was going out of Jerusalem.

29
00:03:52,506 --> 00:03:58,589
[Reader] Now Ahijah had wrapped himself in a new cloak, and the two of them were
alone in the open field.

30
00:03:58,639 --> 00:04:10,541
[Reader] And Ahijah took hold of the new cloak he was wearing, tore it into twelve
pieces, and said to Jeroboam, “Take ten pieces for yourself, for this is what the
LORD,

31
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:19,227
[Reader] the God of Israel, says: ‘Behold, I will tear the kingdom out of the hand
of Solomon, and I will give you ten tribes.

32
00:04:19,277 --> 00:04:31,072
[Reader] If you listen to all that I command you, walk in My ways, and do what is
right in My sight in order to keep My statutes and commandments as My servant David
did,

33
00:04:31,122 --> 00:04:32,838
[Reader] then I will be with you.

34
00:04:32,888 --> 00:04:39,080
[Reader] I will build you a lasting dynasty just as I built for David, and I will
give Israel to you.

35
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,781
It came true, the first half of it.

36
00:04:45,831 --> 00:04:56,306
When Solomon died and his son Rehoboam would not lighten the people’s load, ten
tribes walked away from David’s house and made Jeroboam their king.

37
00:04:56,356 --> 00:05:03,922
The torn cloak had told the truth. But the promise had a second half, and the second
half had a condition.

38
00:05:03,972 --> 00:05:14,488
If you listen to all that I command you, the LORD had said through Ahijah, and walk
in My ways as My servant David did, then I will be with you,

39
00:05:14,538 --> 00:05:19,331
and I will build you a lasting dynasty, just as I built for David.

40
00:05:19,381 --> 00:05:26,490
A house that would last. That was on offer to Jeroboam, and it was his to keep or to
lose.

41
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:31,911
He lost it quickly, and the text tells you how he reasoned.

42
00:05:31,961 --> 00:05:42,856
He said in his heart that if his people kept going up to Jerusalem to worship, their
hearts would go back to the house of David, and they would kill him.

43
00:05:42,906 --> 00:05:48,111
So he did not trust the promise. He protected himself instead.

44
00:05:48,161 --> 00:06:00,407
He made two golden calves and told Israel that going up to Jerusalem was too much
for them: here, O Israel, are your gods, who brought you up out of Egypt.

45
00:06:00,457 --> 00:06:10,522
He set one calf at Bethel, on the southern road, and one at Dan, in the far north,
so that nobody had to pass through Judah to worship.

46
00:06:10,572 --> 00:06:18,063
He built shrines on the high places. He made priests out of anyone who wanted the
job, Levite or not.

47
00:06:18,113 --> 00:06:23,618
He invented a feast of his own and set it a month after the real one.

48
00:06:23,668 --> 00:06:35,020
And when a man of God came to the altar at Bethel and cried out against it, and the
king’s hand withered as he pointed at him, he still did not turn.

49
00:06:35,070 --> 00:06:47,790
Even after that, the text says, Jeroboam did not repent. Through all of it, the old
prophet at Shiloh who had given him the kingdom heard nothing from him.

50
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:51,250
Jeroboam never sent to Ahijah about any of that.

51
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:58,118
Then his son fell ill. The boy’s name was Abijah.

52
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:09,086
He was the heir, and he was, the text will say before this chapter is out, the only
one in that whole house in whom the LORD found any good.

53
00:07:09,136 --> 00:07:18,744
And now, for the first time in years, Jeroboam sends to Shiloh. Not to the calves he
made, not to the priests he appointed.

54
00:07:18,794 --> 00:07:29,833
To the prophet whose word had come true before. The prophet is old by now, and the
text says plainly that he cannot see. Where this is.

55
00:07:29,883 --> 00:07:37,336
Stand at Shiloh again, where we stood last time, and face north, with north at
twelve o'clock.

56
00:07:37,386 --> 00:07:43,396
Eli's gate is somewhere near you, and the house of the LORD is long gone from here.

57
00:07:43,446 --> 00:07:52,301
The queen comes from Tirzah, Jeroboam's city, about a day's walk at one o'clock, and
that is the road the footsteps are on.

58
00:07:52,351 --> 00:07:58,597
Bethel, where one of the golden calves stands, is half a day behind you at seven
o'clock.

59
00:07:58,647 --> 00:08:07,948
And a day's walk at six o'clock is Jerusalem, with Solomon's Temple in it, the house
the king has talked his people out of going up to.

60
00:08:07,998 --> 00:08:12,370
The queen's road comes down from the north and ends at a blind man's door.

61
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:18,810
First Kings chapter 14, verses 1 to 6.

62
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:31,492
[Reader] At that time Abijah son of Jeroboam became ill, and Jeroboam said to his
wife, “Now get up, disguise yourself so they will not recognize you as my wife,

63
00:08:31,542 --> 00:08:32,855
[Reader] and go to Shiloh.

64
00:08:32,905 --> 00:08:38,949
[Reader] For Ahijah the prophet is there; it was he who spoke about my kingship over
this people.

65
00:08:38,999 --> 00:08:47,956
[Reader] Take with you ten loaves of bread, some cakes, and a jar of honey, and go
to him. He will tell you what will become of the boy.”

66
00:08:48,006 --> 00:09:00,751
[Reader] Jeroboam’s wife did as instructed; she arose and went to Shiloh and arrived
at Ahijah’s house. Now Ahijah could not see, for his eyes were dim because of his
age.

67
00:09:00,801 --> 00:09:09,702
[Reader] But the LORD had said to Ahijah, “Behold, the wife of Jeroboam is coming to
ask you about her son, for he is ill.

68
00:09:09,752 --> 00:09:16,051
[Reader] You are to say such and such to her, because when she arrives, she will be
disguised.”

69
00:09:16,101 --> 00:09:27,310
[Reader] So when Ahijah heard the sound of her feet entering the door, he said,
“Come in, wife of Jeroboam! Why are you disguised? For I have been sent to you with
bad news.

70
00:09:30,860 --> 00:09:36,987
Such and such. That is the writer’s shorthand, not God’s words.

71
00:09:37,037 --> 00:09:45,792
He uses it a handful of times in these books when a speech is about to be given in
full, and this one you will hear in a moment, from Ahijah’s mouth,

72
00:09:45,842 --> 00:09:47,742
when she is standing in the door.

73
00:09:47,792 --> 00:09:59,837
And notice who the disguise is for. Jeroboam says it himself: so they will not
recognize you. They. The people on the road and in the town.

74
00:09:59,887 --> 00:10:09,137
A king's wife walking into a village that used to be the holy place, asking a
prophet about the king's heir, would be talked about by nightfall,

75
00:10:09,187 --> 00:10:11,011
and he did not want the talk.

76
00:10:11,061 --> 00:10:22,054
The disguise was never aimed at the prophet. It was aimed at eyes, and the one man
it was carried to had none. Then notice the order of the verses.

77
00:10:22,104 --> 00:10:31,211
Verse 4 says Ahijah could not see. Verse 5 says the LORD had already told him who
was coming and that she would be disguised.

78
00:10:31,261 --> 00:10:43,342
Verse 6 says he heard her feet and named her. The text puts the blindness, the
telling, and the hearing in a row, so that you cannot miss which one did the work.

79
00:10:43,392 --> 00:10:46,410
He did not see through her. He was told.

80
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:59,510
Now imagine the walk, because the text gives us the frame and not the road. She
arose and went to Shiloh. A day, from Tirzah, mostly uphill.

81
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:06,234
She has done this carefully. A plain cloak, the kind a farmer's wife wears to
market.

82
00:11:06,284 --> 00:11:14,565
On her arm a basket, we will suppose, with the gift the king told her to take: ten
loaves, some cakes, a jar of honey.

83
00:11:14,615 --> 00:11:22,510
The old books read that gift as part of the disguise, a poor woman's present to a
holy man, nothing a queen would carry.

84
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:26,873
She passes people on the road and no one looks twice, which is the point.

85
00:11:26,923 --> 00:11:38,409
All day she is a woman nobody recognizes, and all day the thing she is afraid of is
a face: someone from the king's house, someone from the old days,

86
00:11:38,459 --> 00:11:40,732
someone who will say her name.

87
00:11:40,782 --> 00:11:45,628
She reaches Shiloh. She asks for the prophet's house and is pointed to it.

88
00:11:45,678 --> 00:11:58,781
She goes up the path, and stops at the door, and the first thing she sees in the dim
room is the old man's face, and his eyes. They are open. They are not moving.

89
00:11:58,831 --> 00:12:02,415
They are not looking at her, or at anything.

90
00:12:02,465 --> 00:12:11,290
And in that half second, before he says a word, she knows that everything she did to
her face this morning was wasted on the one man she did it for.

91
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:19,530
[Ahijah] Come in, wife of Jeroboam. Why are you disguised? For I have been sent to
you with bad news.

92
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:36,704
Sent. He does not say I knew you, or I heard you. He says I have been sent to you.
And then he gives her the message as it was given to him, for her husband.

93
00:12:36,754 --> 00:12:38,370
Verses 7 to 9.

94
00:12:38,930 --> 00:12:51,425
[Reader] Go, tell Jeroboam that this is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘I
raised you up from among the people and appointed you ruler over My people Israel.

95
00:12:51,475 --> 00:12:55,493
[Reader] I tore the kingdom away from the house of David and gave it to you.

96
00:12:55,543 --> 00:13:06,166
[Reader] But you have not been like My servant David, who kept My commandments and
followed Me with all his heart, doing only what was right in My eyes.

97
00:13:06,216 --> 00:13:09,896
[Reader] You have done more evil than all who came before you.

98
00:13:09,946 --> 00:13:17,520
[Reader] You have proceeded to make for yourself other gods and molten images to
provoke Me, and you have flung Me behind your back.

99
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:26,483
The next two verses are the end of Jeroboam's house, in the plainest terms the Bible
has for such things,

100
00:13:26,533 --> 00:13:31,921
and we will not go into the gruesome details of the destruction of a family.

101
00:13:31,971 --> 00:13:43,530
Then the prophet turns from the king to the woman at the door, and to the reason she
came. Verses 12 and 13, and then 15, because it reaches a long way.

102
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:55,650
[Reader] As for you, get up and go home. When your feet enter the city, the child
will die. All Israel will mourn for him and bury him.

103
00:13:55,700 --> 00:14:07,127
[Reader] For this is the only one belonging to Jeroboam who will receive a proper
burial, because only in him has the LORD, the God of Israel,

104
00:14:07,177 --> 00:14:10,579
[Reader] found any good in the house of Jeroboam.

105
00:14:10,629 --> 00:14:15,469
[Reader] For the LORD will strike Israel as a reed is shaken in the water.

106
00:14:15,519 --> 00:14:26,636
[Reader] He will uproot Israel from this good land that He gave their fathers, and
He will scatter them beyond the Euphrates, because they have made their Asherah
poles,

107
00:14:26,686 --> 00:14:28,590
[Reader] provoking the LORD to anger.

108
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:36,307
Beyond the Euphrates. That is the first time in the Bible anyone says it.

109
00:14:36,357 --> 00:14:44,847
Two hundred years before it happened to the north, and three hundred and fifty
before Zedekiah was marched down that river,

110
00:14:44,897 --> 00:14:55,053
a blind man in Shiloh said the word exile to a woman at his door, and the map we
drew in the first episode of this series was already in his mouth.

111
00:14:55,103 --> 00:14:59,857
When your feet enter the city. Her feet again.

112
00:14:59,907 --> 00:15:09,898
The chapter is built on them: the king tells her to get up and go, she gets up and
goes, the blind man hears her feet at his door,

113
00:15:09,948 --> 00:15:16,850
and the word he gives her is that when those same feet cross into Tirzah her boy
will die.

114
00:15:16,900 --> 00:15:21,721
She asked nothing. The text never gives her a word.

115
00:15:21,771 --> 00:15:31,549
She came to a man who could not see her to find out whether her son would live, and
he told her, and he told her more than she asked,

116
00:15:31,599 --> 00:15:35,022
and the only tenderness in it is the last line.

117
00:15:35,072 --> 00:15:43,910
The boy would be mourned, and buried, and he alone, because in him alone the LORD
had found something good.

118
00:15:47,099 --> 00:15:48,910
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

119
00:15:49,710 --> 00:15:57,396
[Brian] When I started on this story I thought it would be the one where I talked
about our other senses, and how much people underestimate what a blind man hears.

120
00:15:57,446 --> 00:16:03,778
[Brian] The text turned that around on me. Ahijah did not hear who she was. He did
not work it out from anything.

121
00:16:03,828 --> 00:16:13,285
[Brian] The LORD told him before she arrived who was coming and that she would be
disguised, and when he heard feet at the door he said her name. That is how he knew.

122
00:16:13,335 --> 00:16:24,857
[Brian] Not sharper ears. He was told. The word for his eyes is the same one used
for Eli at the gate in the same town: his eyes stood. Set. Still.

123
00:16:24,907 --> 00:16:32,728
[Brian] The translators cannot settle on it, dim in one verse and set or fixed in
the other, but the picture is plain enough to me.

124
00:16:32,778 --> 00:16:41,595
[Brian] Eyes that are still are eyes that are not tracking anything, and are not
seeing much or anything at all. I am getting more this way the longer I am blind.

125
00:16:41,645 --> 00:16:46,865
[Brian] I do not use my eyes for anything now, and they have stopped moving the way
eyes do when they are looking.

126
00:16:46,915 --> 00:16:55,283
[Brian] It is the one description of blindness in this series written from the
outside, of what another person sees when they look at a blind man's face.

127
00:16:55,333 --> 00:17:03,873
[Brian] The queen saw that face when she walked in, and whatever her husband had
told her, she would have known then that the disguise was wasted on him.

128
00:17:03,923 --> 00:17:12,951
[Brian] This story also finishes something that starts back in Genesis. Isaac could
not tell Jacob from Esau. Jacob could not tell Leah from Rachel in the dark.

129
00:17:13,001 --> 00:17:17,887
[Brian] Joseph's brothers stood in front of him in broad daylight and did not
recognize him.

130
00:17:17,937 --> 00:17:24,688
[Brian] All through that family the question is whether a man can tell who is in
front of him, and his eyes, when he has them, are no help.

131
00:17:24,738 --> 00:17:33,281
[Brian] The queen came to Shiloh disguised so that nobody would recognize her, and
the text never says the prophet did. It says he was told.

132
00:17:33,331 --> 00:17:39,369
[Brian] He is the first one in the whole line who did not have to recognize anybody,
and that is the point of him.

133
00:17:39,419 --> 00:17:43,602
[Brian] I did not expect the blind man in this story to be the one who was not
distracted.

134
00:17:43,652 --> 00:17:53,154
[Brian] Jeroboam had his eyes and his kingdom and his calves, and he never once sent
to Ahijah to ask about any of that. He sent when his boy was sick.

135
00:17:53,204 --> 00:18:02,904
[Brian] My lack of vision gives me clarity in a great many areas. I am less
distracted, in a world that is built to pull my attention away from Jesus, where it
needs to be.

136
00:18:02,954 --> 00:18:09,040
[Brian] The old man in Shiloh had nothing to look at, and he was the one person in
the story who could see what was going on,

137
00:18:09,090 --> 00:18:11,820
[Brian] because he was listening to the only voice that knew.

138
00:18:13,870 --> 00:18:22,620
Now the old books. Matthew Henry wrote one sentence about Ahijah that Brian did not
expect to agree with, and does.

139
00:18:23,170 --> 00:18:32,048
[Matthew Henry] He sent to Ahijah the prophet, who lived obscurely and neglected in
Shiloh, blind through age, yet still blest with the visions of the Almighty,

140
00:18:32,098 --> 00:18:40,480
[Matthew Henry] which need not bodily eyes, but are rather favoured by the want of
them, the eyes of the mind being then most intent and least diverted.

141
00:18:41,530 --> 00:18:47,646
Least diverted. Henry also put the king's foolishness in one question.

142
00:18:47,696 --> 00:18:56,625
Could the God of Israel see into the thick darkness of the future, he asks, and not
see through the thin veil of this disguise?

143
00:18:56,675 --> 00:18:59,548
And he noticed what Jeroboam had not done.

144
00:18:59,598 --> 00:19:08,739
He had not sent to Ahijah for advice about setting up his calves, or consecrating
his priests, but had recourse to him in his distress,

145
00:19:08,789 --> 00:19:12,074
when the gods he served could give him no relief.

146
00:19:12,124 --> 00:19:20,289
Henry puts it in a line Brian kept: in trouble have those visited thee who before
slighted thee.

147
00:19:20,339 --> 00:19:27,115
Josephus tells the story the same way as the text, with one addition that is really
just the text said twice.

148
00:19:27,165 --> 00:19:35,320
When she came in, he has Ahijah say to her, you are not hidden from God, who
appeared to me and told me you were coming.

149
00:19:39,870 --> 00:19:56,082
Now the word. Each of these six stories has its own word for eyes that fail, and
they make a chain. Zedekiah's was "ah-VAR," blinded. Isaac's was "kah-HAH," dim.

150
00:19:56,132 --> 00:20:15,117
Jacob's was "kah-VED," heavy. Eli had Isaac's word in the night and a new one at the
gate, and Ahijah has only that last one. His eyes "KOOM," stood.

151
00:20:15,167 --> 00:20:23,052
The same word as Eli's, the same town, and nowhere else in the Bible are eyes
described this way.

152
00:20:23,102 --> 00:20:37,283
It is the ordinary verb for standing up, and this chapter is full of it. Jeroboam
tells his wife to get up and go: that is the word. She arose and went: the word.

153
00:20:37,333 --> 00:20:43,073
Ahijah tells her, get up and go home: the word again.

154
00:20:43,123 --> 00:20:52,487
Everyone in the chapter is rising and going, and in the middle of them is one man
whose eyes have risen and stopped.

155
00:20:52,537 --> 00:21:06,550
The queen's feet went from Tirzah to Shiloh and back. His eyes went nowhere. He knew
more than anyone on the road. So the chain stands at five, with one to go.

156
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:19,720
A king blinded, a father dimmed, a son weighed down, two old men of one town whose
eyes went dim and then stood still.

157
00:21:19,770 --> 00:21:27,873
Five men, and not one of them got his sight back, and not one of them is described
as less than he was.

158
00:21:27,923 --> 00:21:35,500
Next time the last word, and it is the hardest one, and it closes the loop back to
where we began.

159
00:21:37,171 --> 00:21:40,600
What came after. Verses 17 and 18.

160
00:21:41,150 --> 00:21:51,053
[Reader] Then Jeroboam’s wife got up and departed for Tirzah, and as soon as she
stepped over the threshold of the house, the boy died.

161
00:21:51,103 --> 00:22:00,780
[Reader] And they buried him, and all Israel mourned for him, according to the word
that the LORD had spoken through His servant Ahijah the prophet.

162
00:22:01,830 --> 00:22:14,096
The threshold of the house. Not Ahijah's. Her own, in Tirzah, a day later, the word
keeping its time to the step. And the rest of it came as well.

163
00:22:14,146 --> 00:22:27,372
Jeroboam reigned twenty-two years and died, his son reigned two, and a man named
Baasha killed him and every person left of Jeroboam's house, according, the text
says,

164
00:22:27,422 --> 00:22:33,573
to the word that the LORD had spoken through His servant Ahijah the Shilonite.

165
00:22:33,623 --> 00:22:40,132
That is the last time his name appears. An old blind man in a town the glory had
left.

166
00:22:40,182 --> 00:22:47,203
The kingdom’s business came to him twice and he never went looking for it: once on a
road outside Jerusalem,

167
00:22:47,253 --> 00:22:56,304
when Jeroboam was nobody yet and the prophet tore a new cloak into twelve pieces,
and once at his own door, from a king’s wife in disguise.

168
00:22:56,354 --> 00:23:02,820
Once for a kingdom, once for a child. Both times he said exactly what he was given.

169
00:23:06,370 --> 00:23:08,160
Brian, with the last word.

170
00:23:08,710 --> 00:23:16,891
[Brian] People assume a blind man is the easiest person in the room to fool, and
some days they are right. The queen's husband assumed it too.

171
00:23:16,941 --> 00:23:21,746
[Brian] What Ahijah had was not better ears than hers or sharper wits than his.

172
00:23:21,796 --> 00:23:31,670
[Brian] He had been told, by the one voice that is never wrong about who is at the
door, and he believed it before her feet reached the step. That is all that any of
us has.

173
00:23:31,720 --> 00:23:38,660
[Brian] I check what I can, and I am wrong about footsteps all the time. Thankfully,
I am not walking by what my senses can check.

174
00:23:42,210 --> 00:23:52,376
Next time, the last of the six, and the other man the Bible says was bound in bronze
after his eyes were taken.

175
00:23:52,426 --> 00:24:04,417
A hall in Gaza full of people laughing, a boy's hand leading a man by the hand
through the noise, and the man asking to be put where he can feel the pillars.

176
00:24:04,467 --> 00:24:07,360
Samson, between the pillars.

177
00:24:09,910 --> 00:24:18,513
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

178
00:24:18,563 --> 00:24:29,432
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

179
00:24:29,482 --> 00:24:36,660
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

